
<% content_for :title, "On Mood Swings | Bipolar Sober" %>

<style>
  .mood-swings-page {
    background: #080808;
    color: #fff;
    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
  }

  .mood-swings-page *,
  .mood-swings-page *::before,
  .mood-swings-page *::after {
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }

  /* HERO */

  .mood-swings-hero {
    min-height: 85vh;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 80px 6%;
    background-color: #080808;
    background-image:
      linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(8, 8, 8, .65), transparent),
      url("<%= asset_path('bipolar_mood_swings_desktop.png') %>");
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
  }

  .mood-swings-hero-content {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .mood-swings-eyebrow {
    max-width: 440px;
    margin: 0 0 28px;
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.6;
    letter-spacing: .12em;
  }

  .mood-swings-hero h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(58px, 10vw, 150px);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: .88;
    letter-spacing: -.065em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .mood-swings-hero h1 span {
    display: block;
  }

  .mood-swings-intro {
    margin: 32px 0 0;
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.6vw, 22px);
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* QUOTE */

  .mood-swings-quote {
    padding: 48px 6%;
    background: #080808;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .mood-swings-quote blockquote {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .mood-swings-quote blockquote p {
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    color: #eaff00;
    font-size: clamp(30px, 5vw, 64px);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.1;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .mood-swings-quote cite {
    font-size: 14px;
    font-style: normal;
  }

  /* SECTIONS */

  .mood-swings-section {
    padding: 72px 6%;
  }

  .mood-swings-section-inner {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, .85fr) minmax(0, 1.15fr);
    gap: 60px;
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .mood-swings-number {
    margin: 0 0 22px;
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: .12em;
  }

  .mood-swings-section h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(36px, 4.5vw, 68px);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: .95;
    letter-spacing: -.045em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    overflow-wrap: break-word;
  }

  .mood-swings-copy {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .mood-swings-copy p {
    margin: 0 0 22px;
  }

  .mood-swings-copy p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* COLORS */

  .mood-swings-pink {
    background: #ff1493;
    color: #080808;
  }

  .mood-swings-yellow {
    background: #eaff00;
    color: #080808;
  }

  .mood-swings-purple {
    background: #8a00ff;
    color: #fff;
  }

  .mood-swings-black {
    background: #080808;
    color: #fff;
  }

  .mood-swings-white {
    background: #fff;
    color: #080808;
  }

  /* TAKEAWAYS */

  .mood-swings-takeaway {
    padding-top: 22px;
    border-top: 3px solid currentColor;
    font-weight: 800;
  }

  /* FOOTER */

  .mood-swings-footer {
    padding: 40px 6%;
    border-top: 1px solid #444;
    background: #080808;
  }

  .mood-swings-footer-inner {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 24px;
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .mood-swings-footer a {
    color: #eaff00;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.5;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .mood-swings-footer a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .mood-swings-footer a:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid #ff1493;
    outline-offset: 6px;
  }

  /* MOBILE */

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .mood-swings-hero {
      min-height: 88svh;
      align-items: flex-start;
      padding: 48px 6% 60px;
      background-image:
        url("<%= asset_path('bipolar_mood_swings_mobile.png') %>");
      background-position: center bottom;
    }

    .mood-swings-eyebrow {
      margin-bottom: 24px;
      font-size: 10px;
    }

    .mood-swings-hero h1 {
      font-size: clamp(48px, 14vw, 90px);
    }

    .mood-swings-intro {
      margin-top: 24px;
      font-size: 14px;
    }

    .mood-swings-section {
      padding: 48px 6%;
    }

    .mood-swings-section-inner {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 28px;
    }

    .mood-swings-section h2 {
      font-size: clamp(36px, 10vw, 58px);
    }

    .mood-swings-copy {
      font-size: 16px;
    }

    .mood-swings-number {
      margin-bottom: 16px;
    }
  }
</style>


<main class="mood-swings-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="mood-swings-hero"
           aria-labelledby="mood-swings-title">

    <div class="mood-swings-hero-content">

      <p class="mood-swings-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / MOOD SWINGS
      </p>

      <h1 id="mood-swings-title">
        <span>ON</span>
        <span>MOOD</span>
        <span>SWINGS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="mood-swings-intro">
        THE HIGHS. THE LOWS.<br>
        THE EVERYTHING IN BETWEEN.<br>
        AND THE BRAIN THAT CAN'T MAKE UP ITS MIND.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- MEAN GIRLS QUOTE -->
  <section class="mood-swings-quote" aria-label="Opening quote">
    <blockquote>
      <p>"I just have a lot of feelings."</p>
      <cite>— Mean Girls</cite>
    </blockquote>
  </section>


  
<!-- 01 / NORMAL VS. BIPOLAR MOOD SWINGS -->
<section class="mood-swings-section mood-swings-pink">
  <div class="mood-swings-section-inner">

    <div class="mood-swings-section-heading">
      <p class="mood-swings-number">
        01 / NORMAL VS. BIPOLAR
      </p>

      <h2>
        PICK A<br>
        GODDAMN<br>
        MOOD<br>
        ALREADY.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="mood-swings-copy">

      <p>
        Everybody has mood swings. You wake up in a
        good mood, something pisses you off, and
        suddenly you're irritated. You get exciting
        news and feel fantastic. You have a shitty
        conversation and feel miserable. Your
        emotions respond to what's happening
        around you. That's normal.
      </p>

      <p>
        Bipolar disorder is different. It's not
        simply experiencing stronger emotions
        or changing your mind about how you feel.
        Bipolar mood episodes involve significant
        shifts in mood, energy, sleep, activity,
        thinking, and behavior. They can last
        days, weeks, or months, and they can
        substantially change how you function.
      </p>

      <p>
        A normal mood swing might mean feeling
        angry for an afternoon. A bipolar manic
        episode can mean days of sleeping very
        little, racing thoughts, increased energy,
        impulsive spending, and decisions that
        make perfect sense to you at the time
        but absolutely fucking don't later.
      </p>

      <p>
        A normal low might mean feeling sad
        after a disappointing day. Bipolar
        depression can mean losing your energy,
        motivation, and interest in life for
        weeks or months. Your depressive
        episodes have lasted four months
        or longer. That's not a bad Tuesday.
      </p>

      <p>
        And bipolar disorder isn't necessarily
        an endless back-and-forth between
        happiness and sadness. You can experience
        mania without feeling happy, depression
        without constantly crying, and mixed
        symptoms that combine agitation or
        increased energy with depressive feelings.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can also have ordinary emotional
        reactions while living with bipolar
        disorder. Getting angry doesn't
        automatically mean mania. Feeling
        sad doesn't automatically mean
        depression. Sometimes you're just
        having a shitty day.
      </p>

      <p class="mood-swings-takeaway">
        NORMAL MOOD SWINGS CHANGE HOW YOU FEEL.<br>
        BIPOLAR EPISODES CAN CHANGE HOW YOU FUNCTION.
      </p>

    </div>

  </div>
</section>


  <!-- 02 / THE HIGHS -->
  <section class="mood-swings-section mood-swings-yellow">
    <div class="mood-swings-section-inner">

      <div class="mood-swings-section-heading">
        <p class="mood-swings-number">
          02 / THE HIGHS
        </p>

        <h2>
          EVERYTHING.<br>
          ALL AT<br>
          ONCE.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="mood-swings-copy">

        <p>
          When your mood climbs into mania,
          everything can start moving faster.
          Your thoughts race. Your energy increases.
          Ideas feel brilliant. Plans feel urgent.
          You become convinced that whatever
          you're thinking about needs to happen
          right now.
        </p>

        <p>
          Confidence can turn into certainty.
          Impulses become decisions before
          you've had much time to consider
          what comes next. Spending money,
          starting projects, making plans,
          or acting on an idea can feel
          completely reasonable in the moment.
        </p>

        <p>
          The problem isn't necessarily that
          you feel good. Mania doesn't always
          feel good anyway. You can be irritable,
          agitated, restless, or furious.
          What matters is recognizing when
          your mood and energy are changing
          your judgment and behavior.
        </p>

        <p class="mood-swings-takeaway">
          WHEN EVERY IDEA FEELS URGENT,<br>
          URGENCY STOPS MEANING MUCH.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / THE LOWS -->
  <section class="mood-swings-section mood-swings-purple">
    <div class="mood-swings-section-inner">

      <div class="mood-swings-section-heading">
        <p class="mood-swings-number">
          03 / THE LOWS
        </p>

        <h2>
          FOUR<br>
          LONG<br>
          MONTHS.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="mood-swings-copy">

        <p>
          Depression isn't necessarily a few days
          of feeling sad. For you, depressive
          episodes can last four months or longer.
          That's months of exhaustion, heaviness,
          disconnection, and trying to function
          while your mind makes everything
          feel considerably more difficult.
        </p>

        <p>
          Things that normally interest you
          stop being interesting. Motivation
          disappears. Ordinary tasks require
          more effort. You might know perfectly
          well that your life hasn't suddenly
          become worthless, but knowing something
          and feeling it are two different things.
        </p>

        <p>
          And unlike a bad afternoon, you
          can't simply wait until tomorrow
          and assume you'll feel better.
          Depression can settle in and
          stay for months, making even
          familiar parts of your life
          feel distant.
        </p>

        <p class="mood-swings-takeaway">
          SOME MOODS PASS IN HOURS.<br>
          SOME TAKE MONTHS OF YOUR LIFE.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / MIXED SYMPTOMS -->
  <section class="mood-swings-section mood-swings-black">
    <div class="mood-swings-section-inner">

      <div class="mood-swings-section-heading">
        <p class="mood-swings-number">
          04 / MIXED SYMPTOMS
        </p>

        <h2>
          UP.<br>
          DOWN.<br>
          BOTH.<br>
          FUCK.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="mood-swings-copy">

        <p>
          As if mania and depression weren't
          complicated enough separately,
          sometimes symptoms from both
          show up together.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can feel miserable while your
          thoughts race. Exhausted but restless.
          Depressed but agitated. You want
          everything to stop, yet your mind
          refuses to slow down.
        </p>

        <p>
          It's an especially frustrating
          combination because your energy
          and your emotions aren't necessarily
          moving in the same direction.
          You may feel an overwhelming need
          to do something without having
          the clarity to decide what would
          actually help.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't have to force the
          experience into either "high"
          or "low" to recognize that
          something is wrong.
        </p>

        <p class="mood-swings-takeaway">
          YOU CAN FEEL EVERYTHING<br>
          AND NOTHING AT THE SAME TIME.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / THE IN-BETWEEN -->
  <section class="mood-swings-section mood-swings-white">
    <div class="mood-swings-section-inner">

      <div class="mood-swings-section-heading">
        <p class="mood-swings-number">
          05 / YOUR BASELINE
        </p>

        <h2>
          WHAT'S<br>
          NORMAL<br>
          FOR YOU?
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="mood-swings-copy">

        <p>
          Between episodes, you have your own
          ordinary range of emotions, energy,
          habits, and behavior. You aren't
          supposed to feel exactly the same
          every day. Nobody does.
        </p>

        <p>
          Knowing your baseline gives you
          something to compare changes against.
          Maybe you're sleeping less.
          Maybe you're spending more.
          Maybe you've become unusually
          irritable, restless, withdrawn,
          or certain about everything.
        </p>

        <p>
          The important question isn't
          whether you're happy or sad.
          It's whether something has
          shifted significantly from
          what's normal for you.
        </p>

        <p class="mood-swings-takeaway">
          YOU CAN'T RECOGNIZE A CHANGE<br>
          IF YOU DON'T KNOW YOUR BASELINE.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / RECOGNIZING PATTERNS -->
  <section class="mood-swings-section mood-swings-pink">
    <div class="mood-swings-section-inner">

      <div class="mood-swings-section-heading">
        <p class="mood-swings-number">
          06 / RECOGNIZING PATTERNS
        </p>

        <h2>
          PAY<br>
          ATTENTION.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="mood-swings-copy">

        <p>
          You don't need to analyze every
          emotion or spend your entire day
          monitoring your brain. But when
          something starts changing, it's
          worth noticing.
        </p>

        <p>
          Your sleep, energy, mood, thoughts,
          spending, and impulsivity can tell
          you a lot about what's happening.
          One unusual day might mean nothing.
          Several changes happening together
          over time deserve more attention.
        </p>

        <p>
          Physical pain, stress, boredom,
          relationships, and changes in
          routine can also affect how you
          feel. Those circumstances matter,
          even when they don't explain
          everything about a mood episode.
        </p>

        <p>
          Write down what you're noticing
          if it helps. Talk to your psych
          when changes seem significant.
          You don't have to figure out
          everything yourself.
        </p>

        <p class="mood-swings-takeaway">
          NOTICE THE PATTERN.<br>
          NOT JUST THE MOMENT.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 / THE BIGGER PICTURE -->
  <section class="mood-swings-section mood-swings-yellow">
    <div class="mood-swings-section-inner">

      <div class="mood-swings-section-heading">
        <p class="mood-swings-number">
          07 / THE BIGGER PICTURE
        </p>

        <h2>
          SAME BRAIN.<br>
          DIFFERENT<br>
          WEATHER.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="mood-swings-copy">

        <p>
          Living with bipolar disorder means
          understanding that your internal
          world can change dramatically,
          sometimes without your permission
          or a clear explanation.
        </p>

        <p>
          Your moods affect how you experience
          your circumstances, but they don't
          make every thought accurate or
          every impulse worth following.
          You can recognize the influence
          of your illness while still
          taking responsibility for
          your actions.
        </p>

        <p>
          Medication, sleep, awareness,
          and support all have a place
          in managing bipolar disorder.
          None of them guarantee that
          every day will be easy or
          every mood predictable.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't have to treat every
          feeling as a warning sign.
          You also don't have to ignore
          the patterns you've learned
          to recognize.
        </p>

        <p class="mood-swings-takeaway">
          YOUR MOOD CAN CHANGE.<br>
          YOUR ENTIRE LIFE DOESN'T HAVE TO FOLLOW.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- NAVIGATION -->
  <nav class="mood-swings-footer" aria-label="Bipolar topic navigation">
    <div class="mood-swings-footer-inner">

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO BIPOLAR",
          bipolar_path %>

      <%= link_to "ON PERSPECTIVES →",
          bipolar_perspectives_path %>

    </div>
  </nav>

</main>
